# ── Rust build output ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only the workspace root has a target/; the leading slash keeps a
# future crate or directory literally named `target` trackable.
/target/
# The vendored submodule builds into its own target/ when examples are
# run from inside it.
/vendor/*/target/

# rustfmt / rustc leftovers
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb

# Cargo.lock IS committed. This workspace ships binaries (wipemark,
# wipemark-cli) and pins gpui by git rev — a reproducible resolve is the
# whole point. Only the backup file cargo writes on `cargo update` is
# ignored.
Cargo.lock.bak

# ── Local spec / design drops (ssd-docs) ─────────────────────────────
# Watchword-sourced specs and working design documents land here so they
# sit next to the code without ever being committed. Anything durable
# belongs in docs/ instead; the canonical copy of a spec is its
# Watchword key.
/ssd-docs/

# ── Agent + editor state ─────────────────────────────────────────────
/.claude/
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo

# ── OS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# ── Product runtime data ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Downloaded GGUF weights and partial downloads. In normal operation
# these live under the OS data dir (see wipemark-models::layout), but a
# dev run with WIPEMARK_DATA_DIR pointed at the repo would drop them
# here — a multi-GB commit is not the way to find that out.
/models/
*.gguf
*.partial

# Config / queue / history written by the app or CLI during development.
*.log
/config.toml
/presets.toml
/queue.json
/history.jsonl

# ── Packaging output ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
/dist/
*.dmg
*.AppImage
*.deb
